WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:04.960
Albert Einstein was so smart that scientists&nbsp;
studied his brain after he died to figure out why.

00:00:05.600 --> 00:00:10.720
He was such an icon that Time magazine&nbsp;
named him “person of the century” in 1999.

00:00:10.720 --> 00:00:15.040
But despite his fame, the world’s&nbsp;
best-known scientist had his secrets.

00:00:15.040 --> 00:00:17.600
The FBI kept a clandestine file on him.

00:00:17.600 --> 00:00:20.240
And he had an illegitimate&nbsp;
child, to name just two.

00:00:20.800 --> 00:00:23.760
This is the story of Albert Einstein in full.

00:00:23.760 --> 00:00:27.520
Einstein was born in Ulm in&nbsp;
southwestern Germany on March 14,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.520 --> 00:00:33.280
1879. The family moved to Munich just a few weeks&nbsp;
later where he grew up with his younger sister.

00:00:33.280 --> 00:00:38.080
When he was five years old, he fell ill one day,&nbsp;
his father gave him a compass to cheer him up.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.080 --> 00:00:40.560
Einstein was amazed that the needle always pointed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.560 --> 00:00:44.160
to the magnetic north no matter&nbsp;
which way he turned the compass.

00:00:44.160 --> 00:00:48.320
In his Autobiographical Notes, he recalled:&nbsp;
“I can still remember—or at least believe I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.320 --> 00:00:52.960
can remember—that this experience made&nbsp;
a deep and lasting impression upon me.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.960 --> 00:00:55.840
Something deeply hidden had to be behind things.”

00:00:55.840 --> 00:00:59.120
So began a lifelong fascination with physics.

00:00:59.680 --> 00:01:03.440
He later said that if he hadn’t become&nbsp;
a physicist he’d probably be a musician.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.440 --> 00:01:06.480
He loved music, especially Mozart&nbsp;
sonatas, and played the violin.

00:01:06.480 --> 00:01:10.080
Unlike many geniuses, Einstein&nbsp;
wasn’t a child prodigy.

00:01:10.080 --> 00:01:14.320
He didn’t speak full sentences until he&nbsp;
was five, according to his biographers.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.320 --> 00:01:19.760
His parents were understandably worried. But when&nbsp;
he got into school, Einstein came into his own.

00:01:19.760 --> 00:01:24.880
His family was Jewish but he attended a Catholic&nbsp;
elementary school where he excelled as a student.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.880 --> 00:01:29.040
There was a rumor going around that he flunked&nbsp;
math but to the contrary, Einstein said that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.040 --> 00:01:33.520
he had already mastered differential and&nbsp;
integral calculus before the age of 15.

00:01:34.080 --> 00:01:38.720
For a while, his father, Hermann, ran a small&nbsp;
electrochemical plant but he struggled to keep&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.720 --> 00:01:43.600
it going. Eventually, he moved his family to&nbsp;
Italy in the hopes of finding new opportunities.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.600 --> 00:01:46.160
Young Albert stayed behind to finish high school.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.160 --> 00:01:50.160
He hated school where success depended&nbsp;
on memorization and obeying authority.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.160 --> 00:01:54.000
He was a rebel and apparently threw&nbsp;
temper tantrums. One exasperated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.000 --> 00:01:59.520
teacher even said he would never amount to&nbsp;
anything. Einstein dropped out at age 15.

00:01:59.520 --> 00:02:04.240
He also renounced his German citizenship which&nbsp;
got him out of mandatory military service.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.240 --> 00:02:07.680
For a few years, he belonged to no&nbsp;
country, and loathed nationalism,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.680 --> 00:02:10.080
preferring to be a citizen of the world.

00:02:10.080 --> 00:02:13.200
When he did eventually become&nbsp;
a citizen again, it was Swiss.

00:02:13.200 --> 00:02:16.720
When he moved to Switzerland, he tried to&nbsp;
get admitted to the prestigious Swiss Federal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.720 --> 00:02:21.760
Institute of Technology. He did well in the math&nbsp;
and physics section of the entrance exam but is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.760 --> 00:02:27.120
said to have done horribly in language, zoology,&nbsp;
and botany. It didn’t help that the exam was in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.120 --> 00:02:31.920
French. So he didn’t get in and instead, continued&nbsp;
his high school studies. He was a good student,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.920 --> 00:02:36.800
scoring the highest possible grade of 6 in&nbsp;
many subjects, including in math and physics.

00:02:36.800 --> 00:02:39.680
With this under his belt, he was&nbsp;
automatically admitted to the Swiss&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.680 --> 00:02:43.120
Federal Institute of Technology, where&nbsp;
he had originally flunked the entrance&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.120 --> 00:02:47.280
exam. He enrolled in a four-year&nbsp;
teaching program in math and physics.

00:02:47.280 --> 00:02:52.560
That’s where he met his future wife Mileva Maric&nbsp;
- the only female student in his physics class.

00:02:52.560 --> 00:02:56.400
Einstein’s private letters discovered&nbsp;
in the eighties caused a sensation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.400 --> 00:03:01.280
because they revealed the couple had a child out&nbsp;
of wedlock, a daughter named Lieserl. According&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.280 --> 00:03:06.080
to their correspondence, Lieserl was born&nbsp;
in 1902, a year before her parents married.

00:03:06.080 --> 00:03:09.920
She was cared for by her mother while staying&nbsp;
with her family in her native Serbia while&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.920 --> 00:03:14.800
Einstein worked in Switzerland. Maric would&nbsp;
later join Einstein without the child.

00:03:14.800 --> 00:03:19.280
It’s unclear what happened to their daughter.&nbsp;
Historians believe she either died in infancy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.280 --> 00:03:22.480
probably from scarlet fever or&nbsp;
was given up for adoption. 
 &nbsp;

00:03:23.200 --> 00:03:26.320
Maric and Einstein would have&nbsp;
two sons after they married.

00:03:26.320 --> 00:03:29.920
The eldest, Hans Albert, said&nbsp;
Einstein wasn’t a good father,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.920 --> 00:03:33.920
remarking: “Probably the only&nbsp;
project he ever gave up on was me.”

00:03:34.640 --> 00:03:39.760
In 1901, Einstein received his diploma to teach&nbsp;
physics and math. But, he struggled to find an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.760 --> 00:03:45.200
academic position after two years of searching. He&nbsp;
is said to have even applied to teach high school.

00:03:45.200 --> 00:03:49.120
Eventually, he worked at the Swiss&nbsp;
patent office in Bern for seven years.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.120 --> 00:03:52.880
This job would be a blessing in&nbsp;
disguise. It wasn’t mentally challenging,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.880 --> 00:03:55.760
and he found that when he was done&nbsp;
evaluating patents for the day,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.760 --> 00:03:59.840
he could use the rest of his time to work&nbsp;
on scientific research - his real passion.

00:04:00.480 --> 00:04:05.680
1905 was Einstein’s year of miracles. He&nbsp;
wrote four papers in a German scientific&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.680 --> 00:04:08.800
journal that changed the way we see the universe.

00:04:08.800 --> 00:04:12.560
I’ll try to explain them as simply as&nbsp;
possible. Einstein is believed to have said:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.560 --> 00:04:16.240
“If you can't explain it simply, you&nbsp;
don't understand it well enough.”

00:04:16.240 --> 00:04:21.040
The first was his theory of light. Physicists&nbsp;
at the time believed light was a wave.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.040 --> 00:04:25.440
But it didn’t make sense that light could&nbsp;
create an electric current. Einstein proposed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.440 --> 00:04:30.400
that light was actually made up of a stream&nbsp;
of particles called photons, and these photons&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.400 --> 00:04:36.080
could knock an electron off an atom to create&nbsp;
a current. This is the Photoelectric Effect.

00:04:36.080 --> 00:04:40.480
His second paper made a case for the existence&nbsp;
of atoms. He observed what seemed like the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.480 --> 00:04:44.480
random movement of particles in water and&nbsp;
reasoned that it’s not so random if the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:44.480 --> 00:04:49.280
water is actually made up of invisible atoms&nbsp;
that cause the particles to jiggle. This was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:49.280 --> 00:04:54.320
called Brownian Motion after the botanist Robert&nbsp;
Brown who had observed the phenomenon earlier.

00:04:54.320 --> 00:04:58.720
His third and most famous discovery is his&nbsp;
Special Theory of Relativity. In which it’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.720 --> 00:05:04.000
impossible to say if two events occurred at the&nbsp;
same time if those events are separated in space.

00:05:04.000 --> 00:05:06.640
A, B, and C all happen at the same time, however,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:06.640 --> 00:05:11.040
they appear to occur in a different order&nbsp;
depending on the location of the observer.

00:05:11.040 --> 00:05:15.360
Or let’s say someone in London starts running&nbsp;
at the same time as someone in New York.&nbsp;

00:05:15.360 --> 00:05:18.400
They would appear to start running&nbsp;
at different times if the observer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.400 --> 00:05:21.520
is on a plane flying between London and New York.

00:05:21.520 --> 00:05:25.120
Relativity is the basis of the&nbsp;
world’s most famous formula.

00:05:25.120 --> 00:05:27.280
E = mc squared.

00:05:27.280 --> 00:05:29.600
His fourth paper gave us this equation which shows&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:29.600 --> 00:05:33.040
how energy equals mass times&nbsp;
the speed of light squared.&nbsp;

00:05:33.040 --> 00:05:37.440
In case you’re feeling somewhat intellectually&nbsp;
challenged at this point, Einstein once said:

00:05:37.440 --> 00:05:40.080
“Do not worry about your&nbsp;
difficulties in Mathematics.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:40.080 --> 00:05:42.720
I can assure you mine are still greater.”

00:05:42.720 --> 00:05:47.280
So under the right conditions, energy can&nbsp;
become mass and mass can become energy.

00:05:47.280 --> 00:05:51.360
Here’s an example. Take a paper clip. To&nbsp;
find out how much energy is inside of it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:51.360 --> 00:05:54.240
you’d multiply its mass by&nbsp;
the speed of light squared.

00:05:54.880 --> 00:05:59.680
If you could turn every one of the atoms in this&nbsp;
paper clip into pure energy leaving no mass,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:59.680 --> 00:06:04.000
then this paper clip would be as powerful&nbsp;
as the bomb that destroyed Hiroshima.

00:06:04.000 --> 00:06:06.000
Indeed, physicists began to consider whether&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:06.000 --> 00:06:10.000
his equation might actually&nbsp;
make an atomic bomb possible.

00:06:10.000 --> 00:06:14.160
As Einstein gained greater prestige,&nbsp;
his wife gained more household work.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:14.160 --> 00:06:18.160
Maric was also passionate about math and&nbsp;
science but gave up her own ambitions to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:18.160 --> 00:06:21.440
care for their children. There is&nbsp;
even some evidence she helped her&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:21.440 --> 00:06:25.840
husband develop some of the concepts in his&nbsp;
famous papers. In Einstein’s letters to her,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:25.840 --> 00:06:30.160
he referenced “...bringing our work on&nbsp;
relative motion to a successful conclusion!"

00:06:30.720 --> 00:06:36.240
The papers would transform him from an unknown&nbsp;
patent office to a renowned genius. He would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:36.240 --> 00:06:40.800
go on to teach physics at universities in&nbsp;
Prague, Zurich, and Berlin where he reacquired&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:40.800 --> 00:06:46.160
German citizenship and spent his time during&nbsp;
World War I. War, by the way, disgusted him.

00:06:46.160 --> 00:06:51.120
Although his professional life was going well,&nbsp;
his personal life suffered. Einstein would write&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:51.120 --> 00:06:55.760
to his first love. Marie Winteler whom he met&nbsp;
as a teenager. He spilled his heart to her,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:55.760 --> 00:06:59.200
saying how much he missed her and how he&nbsp;
thought of her whenever he had a free moment.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:59.200 --> 00:07:02.880
It was perhaps no surprise when he&nbsp;
and his wife eventually split up.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:02.880 --> 00:07:06.320
Maric took the boys back to Zurich&nbsp;
while Einstein remained in Berlin.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:06.320 --> 00:07:12.240
They divorced in 1919 and immediately after,&nbsp;
Einstein married his first cousin, Elsa Lowenthal,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:12.240 --> 00:07:17.120
with whom he had been having an affair for years.&nbsp;
He would later end up cheating on Elsa as well.

00:07:17.680 --> 00:07:22.240
As part of the divorce agreement with Maric she&nbsp;
would receive money from a Nobel prize if he were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:22.240 --> 00:07:28.080
ever to win one. And he did, in 1921, for his&nbsp;
theory of light - not his theory of relativity.

00:07:28.080 --> 00:07:31.920
There was something about his relativity&nbsp;
theory that kept nagging at him because it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:31.920 --> 00:07:36.880
didn’t acknowledge the existence of gravity.&nbsp;
So he tinkered with it for ten years before&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:36.880 --> 00:07:41.040
coming up with his General Theory of&nbsp;
Relativity which completed the picture.

00:07:41.600 --> 00:07:44.800
200 years before Einstein, Sir&nbsp;
Isaac Newton provided the world&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:44.800 --> 00:07:48.160
with insight into gravity but&nbsp;
didn’t explain how it worked.

00:07:48.160 --> 00:07:50.640
How is it that the sun pulls on the Earth?

00:07:50.640 --> 00:07:54.720
Einstein’s General Theory of Relativity&nbsp;
says something heavy, like the sun,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:54.720 --> 00:07:59.680
causes a warp or a dent in space. The&nbsp;
Earth is impacted by the dent and rolls&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:59.680 --> 00:08:03.840
around the sun like a marble rolling&nbsp;
in a bowl - in other words, orbits.

00:08:03.840 --> 00:08:08.720
His theory also suggested that light from&nbsp;
another star would be bent by the sun’s gravity.

00:08:08.720 --> 00:08:13.040
If this were true, then starlight passing&nbsp;
by the sun would be bent so that we on Earth&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:13.040 --> 00:08:17.120
would think that the apparent location of&nbsp;
the star is different than it really is.

00:08:17.120 --> 00:08:21.360
This theory was considered to be preposterous&nbsp;
at the time and could only be proven during&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:21.360 --> 00:08:25.520
a total solar eclipse when the moon&nbsp;
blocks out the bright light of the sun.

00:08:25.520 --> 00:08:28.880
And that’s exactly what happened in 1919.

00:08:28.880 --> 00:08:32.800
English astronomer Sir Arthur Eddington&nbsp;
traveled to the coast of West Africa to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:32.800 --> 00:08:38.320
photograph the total solar eclipse. The&nbsp;
sun had in fact deflected the light.

00:08:38.320 --> 00:08:40.960
Recently, Einstein was proved right once again.

00:08:41.520 --> 00:08:46.240
Stanford University astrophysicists saw&nbsp;
light behind a black hole for the first time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:46.240 --> 00:08:50.160
which is strange because black holes&nbsp;
have such a strong gravitational pull&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:50.160 --> 00:08:54.000
that light cannot escape them. The&nbsp;
reason we can see light is that the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:54.000 --> 00:08:59.040
black hole is warping space and bending&nbsp;
light as predicted by general relativity.

00:08:59.040 --> 00:09:04.560
Einstein became a celebrity overnight. Instantly&nbsp;
recognizable thanks to immense press coverage&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:04.560 --> 00:09:08.080
even though his theory meant very&nbsp;
little to the average person. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:08.080 --> 00:09:11.680
world needed something to celebrate&nbsp;
after a long and horrifying war.

00:09:12.240 --> 00:09:15.120
He began traveling abroad,&nbsp;
going to Asia, the Middle East,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:15.120 --> 00:09:19.120
and the U.S. where he gave lectures&nbsp;
at Columbia University and Princeton.

00:09:19.120 --> 00:09:23.040
He published an essay on his first&nbsp;
impression of America in 1921,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:23.040 --> 00:09:27.200
noting: “What strikes a visitor is&nbsp;
the joyous, positive attitude to life.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:27.200 --> 00:09:31.840
The American is friendly, self-confident,&nbsp;
optimistic, and without envy."

00:09:31.840 --> 00:09:36.480
He would make more trips to the U.S., and&nbsp;
while on one of these visits, in early 1933,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:36.480 --> 00:09:40.560
he came to the stark realization that&nbsp;
he could never return to Germany.

00:09:40.560 --> 00:09:45.600
The Nazis had come to power under Adolf Hitler&nbsp;
and Einstein was everything the dictator hated.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:45.600 --> 00:09:49.600
He was Jewish, he was part of the&nbsp;
intelligentsia, and he was a pacifist.

00:09:49.600 --> 00:09:53.440
A German magazine listed him as an&nbsp;
enemy of the regime with the caption:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:53.440 --> 00:09:57.440
Not Yet Hanged and reportedly put&nbsp;
a $5,000 price tag on his head.

00:09:58.000 --> 00:10:01.280
When he returned to Europe, he went to&nbsp;
Belgium where he renounced his German&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:01.280 --> 00:10:05.600
citizenship. He then traveled to England.&nbsp;
Einstein had offers to teach at Oxford&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:05.600 --> 00:10:09.760
and several European universities&nbsp;
but chose to emigrate to the U.S.

00:10:09.760 --> 00:10:14.240
He took up a faculty position at the Institute&nbsp;
for Advanced Study in Princeton, New Jersey&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:14.240 --> 00:10:19.200
where he remained for the rest of his life. Many&nbsp;
prestigious American universities like Harvard&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:19.200 --> 00:10:24.240
and Yale had very few Jewish faculty or students&nbsp;
back then as a result of discriminatory quotas.

00:10:24.240 --> 00:10:28.880
By the time he had arrived in the U.S., his&nbsp;
best scientific research was already behind him.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:28.880 --> 00:10:32.240
Nothing would trump his theory of&nbsp;
relativity or his other earlier work.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:32.240 --> 00:10:37.120
He tried and failed to find one equation&nbsp;
to explain all of the forces of nature.

00:10:37.120 --> 00:10:42.480
Despite the stability he found at the research&nbsp;
institution, life wasn’t always easy for Einstein.

00:10:42.480 --> 00:10:47.760
His younger son Eduard was diagnosed with&nbsp;
schizophrenia and suffered his first breakdown.

00:10:47.760 --> 00:10:52.960
His wife Elsa suffered from a painful&nbsp;
illness that would take her life in 1936.

00:10:52.960 --> 00:10:54.960
And to his horror, scientists began to look at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:54.960 --> 00:10:59.120
whether his equation E = mc squared&nbsp;
could in fact make an atomic bomb.

00:10:59.680 --> 00:11:04.880
Einstein detested war. He had dedicated much&nbsp;
of his time to writing about peace. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:04.880 --> 00:11:09.920
despite being a pacifist, he was alarmed by the&nbsp;
rise of fascism and signed a letter to President&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:09.920 --> 00:11:16.000
Roosevelt in 1939, warning him that the Nazis&nbsp;
were working on an atomic bomb. This led Roosevelt&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:16.000 --> 00:11:21.280
to set up the Manhattan Project - the secret&nbsp;
American-led effort to develop an atomic weapon.

00:11:21.280 --> 00:11:24.240
Einstein’s formula was key to its success.

00:11:24.240 --> 00:11:30.080
But he wasn’t involved with it himself because the&nbsp;
FBI didn’t trust him. Washington considered him&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:30.080 --> 00:11:35.120
a security risk because of his association with&nbsp;
the peace movement and socialist organizations.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:35.120 --> 00:11:40.720
The FBI kept tabs on him - they had a&nbsp;
dossier that grew to over 1,400 files.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:40.720 --> 00:11:44.720
FBI director J. Edgar Hoover even&nbsp;
recommended he be kept out of America&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:44.720 --> 00:11:48.960
under the Alien Exclusion Act though this&nbsp;
was overruled by the State Department.

00:11:48.960 --> 00:11:51.760
Einstein was also a civil rights activist.

00:11:51.760 --> 00:11:55.280
As a Jewish scientist who&nbsp;
experienced anti-Semitism in Germany,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:55.280 --> 00:11:58.480
he was taken back by racial&nbsp;
segregation in the U.S.

00:11:58.480 --> 00:12:03.840
In 1946 he traveled to Lincoln University,&nbsp;
a small university in Pennsylvania that was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:03.840 --> 00:12:07.360
the first to grant degrees to blacks.&nbsp;
He gave a speech in which he called&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:07.360 --> 00:12:12.000
racism “...a disease of white people.&nbsp;
I do not intend to be quiet about it.”

00:12:12.000 --> 00:12:16.320
Yet, it appears, he was not immune to&nbsp;
the disease. His private diaries would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:16.320 --> 00:12:21.520
reveal prejudiced attitudes toward other&nbsp;
races. In the 1920s he traveled throughout&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:21.520 --> 00:12:25.920
Asia and wrote that the Chinese were an&nbsp;
“industrious, filthy, obtuse people.”

00:12:25.920 --> 00:12:29.520
“It would be a pity if these&nbsp;
Chinese supplant all other races.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:29.520 --> 00:12:32.720
For the likes of us the mere&nbsp;
thought is unspeakably dreary.”

00:12:33.360 --> 00:12:37.440
These words are in stark&nbsp;
contrast to his public image.

00:12:37.440 --> 00:12:41.280
In 1940, Einstein became a&nbsp;
citizen of the United States.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:41.280 --> 00:12:46.320
His commitment to the cause of peace led him to&nbsp;
champion the creation of a one-world government.

00:12:46.320 --> 00:12:51.360
He initially rejected the idea of a Jewish&nbsp;
state on the grounds of detesting nationalism.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:51.360 --> 00:12:55.360
However, after seeing the persecution of&nbsp;
Jews in Europe, he promoted the Zionist&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:55.360 --> 00:12:59.760
cause of a Jewish nation even though&nbsp;
he was personally torn over the issue.

00:12:59.760 --> 00:13:04.880
Einstein would say: “My relationship to the&nbsp;
Jewish people has become my strongest human bond”&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:04.880 --> 00:13:06.080
though he wasn’t religious,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:06.080 --> 00:13:10.160
he didn’t believe in a personal God&nbsp;
and preferred to be called an agnostic.

00:13:10.160 --> 00:13:13.360
The State of Israel was created in 1948.

00:13:13.360 --> 00:13:19.440
And in 1952, Israel’s prime minister David&nbsp;
Ben-Gurion offered Einstein the post of president.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:19.440 --> 00:13:22.560
Einstein declined, explaining that&nbsp;
he had spent his life dealing with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:22.560 --> 00:13:27.120
objective matters and lacked the aptitude&nbsp;
and experience of dealing with people.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:27.120 --> 00:13:29.840
He was smart enough to know his own shortcomings.

00:13:30.400 --> 00:13:36.400
On April 17, 1955, Einstein checked himself&nbsp;
into Princeton Hospital in New Jersey after his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:36.400 --> 00:13:41.360
abdominal aortic aneurysm burst. The aorta&nbsp;
is the largest blood vessel in the body.

00:13:41.360 --> 00:13:43.680
He had previously had surgery on it.

00:13:43.680 --> 00:13:45.600
This time, he refused surgery,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:45.600 --> 00:13:49.520
saying it was tasteless to prolong&nbsp;
life. And that it was time to go.

00:13:50.400 --> 00:13:55.600
He died the next day, mumbling a few words in&nbsp;
German before taking his last breath. The nurse&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:55.600 --> 00:14:00.320
on duty didn’t understand German and couldn’t&nbsp;
repeat it so what he said is lost to history.

00:14:00.880 --> 00:14:03.280
Einstein’s remains were cremated and his ashes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:03.280 --> 00:14:06.240
scattered in a secret spot&nbsp;
along the Delaware River.

00:14:06.240 --> 00:14:09.200
Those ashes didn’t include his brain.

00:14:09.200 --> 00:14:12.560
A pathologist named Thomas Harvey&nbsp;
removed his brain so that scientists&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:12.560 --> 00:14:15.120
could try to figure out why he was so intelligent.

00:14:15.120 --> 00:14:17.040
But he did so without permission!

00:14:17.040 --> 00:14:18.880
After Einstein’s family members found out,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:18.880 --> 00:14:22.880
he eventually got the okay to use&nbsp;
the brain for scientific research.

00:14:22.880 --> 00:14:27.280
Einstein’s brain was found to be missing a&nbsp;
bordering region called the lateral sulcus&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:27.280 --> 00:14:31.680
which researchers believe may have led neurons&nbsp;
in this part of the brain to communicate better.

00:14:32.240 --> 00:14:36.400
Scientists also believe his neurons used up&nbsp;
more energy because his brain was found to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:36.400 --> 00:14:41.920
have a higher percentage of glial cells that&nbsp;
nourish, support, and protect the neurons.

00:14:41.920 --> 00:14:44.240
The pathologist then cut up the brain into pieces,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:44.240 --> 00:14:48.480
stored it into jars preserved in&nbsp;
formaldehyde, and kept it in his basement.

00:14:48.480 --> 00:14:54.480
Today, the most celebrated brain in the world is&nbsp;
in pieces at The Mütter Museum in Philadelphia.

00:14:54.480 --> 00:14:57.360
It’s hard to fathom what Einstein&nbsp;
would think of all this attention.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:57.360 --> 00:15:01.920
He specifically wanted to be cremated so&nbsp;
that people wouldn’t worship his body.

00:15:01.920 --> 00:15:07.840
But worshipped he is. The 99th element&nbsp;
of the periodic table is named after him.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:07.840 --> 00:15:10.640
Einsteinium was discovered&nbsp;
shortly after his death.

00:15:11.200 --> 00:15:15.040
Albert Einstein lives on as one&nbsp;
of the smartest people in history&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:15.040 --> 00:15:18.960
who changed the way we view and&nbsp;
understand the world and the universe.

00:15:18.960 --> 00:15:23.040
He was influential in supporting so&nbsp;
many other causes besides science.

00:15:23.600 --> 00:15:25.840
He lived an extraordinary life.

